<%#
  GET /studio/privacy (dashboard/routes/legal-pages.js). Locals: user, facts (STUDIO_FACTS).
  Every statement was checked against desktop/studio/src on 2026-11-19: config.js (settings, secrets), local-key.js,
  logger.js (redaction, 1 MB x 5), engine-client.js (hello, state, event, request), main.js (fitState, defaultDeviceName,
  syncSounds, openExternal), obs.js (events, replay file name only), voice.js, updater.js, obs-config.js, vod-safe.js,
  package.json (dependencies), renderer/index.html (Content-Security-Policy), and electron-updater 6.8.11 AppUpdater.js
  (Phase 5, 0.2.6: lights/*.js, voicemeeter.js, bridge-server.js, sandbox.js, main.js devicesReport() and bridgeHandlers()), and
  (x-user-staging-id; from 0.2.5 updater.js blankStagingId() sends the nil UUID and deletes .updaterId, H-767). Server side: migrations 454-457, core/stream-bot/studio.js (settings.studio), privacy.js.
  tests/legal-studio-pages.test.js re-checks the facts that can drift (defaults, log size, dependencies, retention).
%>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" data-theme="<%= locals.uiTheme || 'dark' %>" data-density="<%= locals.uiDensity || 'comfortable' %>" data-style="<%= locals.uiStyle || 'cards' %>">
<head>
<%- include('partials/site-head', { title: 'CertiFried Studio privacy · CertiFried', description: 'Exactly what CertiFried Studio keeps on your PC, what it sends to CertiFried and why, what never leaves your PC, and how to export, delete, unpair and revoke.', path: '/studio/privacy' }) %>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/cf-studio-pages.css">
</head>
<body class="cfs-body">
<%- include('partials/site-header', { active: '', user: locals.user || null, returnTo: '/studio/privacy' }) %>
<% const _f = locals.facts || {}; %>
    <main id="main-content" tabindex="-1" class="cfs-main">
        <div class="cfs-pagehead">
            <span class="cfs-eyebrow">CertiFried Studio</span>
            <h1>Studio privacy</h1>
            <p class="cfs-lede">What the Windows app keeps on your PC, what it sends to CertiFried and why, and what never leaves your PC. This page covers the app; the <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> covers everything else.</p>
            <p class="cfs-meta">Last updated November 21, 2026 (heart rate, Studio 0.2.14). Checked against Studio's source code.</p>
        </div>
        <%- include('partials/studio-nav', { current: 'privacy' }) %>

        <div class="cfs-doc">
            <nav class="cfs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
                <h2>On this page</h2>
                <ol>
                    <li><a href="#short-version">The short version</a></li>
                    <li><a href="#on-your-pc">Kept on your PC</a></li>
                    <li><a href="#sent">Sent to CertiFried, and why</a></li>
                    <li><a href="#never-leaves">Never leaves your PC</a></li>
                    <li><a href="#no-telemetry">No telemetry</a></li>
                    <li><a href="#on-the-server">Kept on the server</a></li>
                    <li><a href="#export-delete">Export and delete</a></li>
                    <li><a href="#unpair">Unpair, revoke, uninstall</a></li>
                    <li><a href="#third-parties">Third parties</a></li>
                    <li><a href="#changes">Changes to this page</a></li>
                </ol>
            </nav>
            <div class="cfs-prose">
                <h2 id="short-version">The short version</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>Studio has no analytics, telemetry, crash reporting or advertising code.</li>
                    <li>Your OBS password and your microphone audio never leave your PC.</li>
                    <li>Studio tells CertiFried the names of your OBS scenes, sources, audio sources and filters, and of your sounds, so the dashboard can offer them in the automation editor. It never sends video, audio or the contents of your files.</li>
                    <li>Studio connects to certifriedmultitool.com, to OBS on your PC, and to web addresses you put in a Play a sound action. Only if you turn them on: to lights on your home network and Logitech Litra lights on USB (after you choose Find lights), to Voicemeeter on this PC, to the CertiFried Stream Deck plugin on this PC, and over Bluetooth to the heart-rate watch or strap you choose under Heart rate. When you first turn on voice commands (Studio 0.2.12 and later), it downloads the speech model once from Hugging Face, where the Whisper project publishes it, and on a PC with an NVIDIA graphics card, NVIDIA's CUDA files from NVIDIA and the graphics card part of Whisper from certifriedmultitool.com. Each file is checked against a checksum built into Studio before it is used. Nothing else.</li>
                    <li>Light pairing tokens, your lights' network addresses and the Stream Deck token stay on your PC.</li>
                    <li>Heart rate (Studio 0.2.14 and later) is read only after you choose a device under Heart rate. Studio sends the readings to CertiFried for your overlay and automations, unless you untick that; the server keeps them in memory only, never in your event log.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2 id="on-your-pc">Kept on your PC</h2>
                <p>Everything is in <code><%= _f.dataDir %></code> unless the table says otherwise.</p>
                <div class="cfs-table-wrap"><table class="cfs-table-stack">
                    <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Where</th><th>How it is protected</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Settings: server address, this PC's device name, OBS address and port, output device and volume, hotkeys, voice options and phrases, app options; the lights Studio found (name, model, network address, what each can do), each Hue bridge's certificate fingerprint, whether Voicemeeter control and the Stream Deck connection are on, and the Stream Deck port; the heart-rate device you chose (its name and Bluetooth address) and whether its readings are sent</td><td data-label="Where"><code>settings.json</code></td><td data-label="Protection">Your Windows account's folder permissions</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Device token (from pairing), OBS WebSocket password, the pairing tokens of your Hue bridges and Nanoleaf panels, and the Stream Deck token</td><td data-label="Where"><code>secrets.json</code></td><td data-label="Protection">Encrypted with Windows' own protection for your account (DPAPI). Never stored in plain text. See the note below.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Fallback key</td><td data-label="Where"><code>local-key.bin</code> (only while DPAPI is not working)</td><td data-label="Protection">A random key, readable only by your Windows user. Deleted once DPAPI works again.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Log files</td><td data-label="Where"><code>logs\studio.log</code> and up to <%= _f.logKeep %> older files of <%= _f.logMb %> MB each</td><td data-label="Protection">Device tokens, pairing codes, passwords and authorisation headers are removed before anything is written</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Your own sounds, and copies of the sounds you uploaded to the dashboard</td><td data-label="Where"><code>Sounds</code> and <code>Dashboard sounds</code></td><td data-label="Protection">Folder permissions</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>The phrase list for voice commands (only with Windows speech recognition)</td><td data-label="Where"><code>voice\voice-phrases.json</code></td><td data-label="Protection">Folder permissions. No audio is ever saved.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>The speech model and, with an NVIDIA graphics card, the graphics card files (about 150 MB, or about 1.2 GB with the graphics card)</td><td data-label="Where"><code>voice\models</code> and <code>voice\cuda-12.9.1</code></td><td data-label="Protection">Folder permissions. Program files and a model, no personal data.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Screenshots an automation takes</td><td data-label="Where"><code><%= _f.screenshotsDir %></code></td><td data-label="Protection">Folder permissions</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Nothing for updates: versions before 0.2.5 kept a random ID from the update component in <code>.updaterId</code>; 0.2.5 and later delete that file and never make one</td><td data-label="Where"><code>.updaterId</code> (removed)</td><td data-label="Protection">Not kept</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Backups Studio makes before changing OBS files</td><td data-label="Where">Next to the file, in OBS's folder (<code>%APPDATA%\obs-studio</code>)</td><td data-label="Protection">Copies of your own OBS settings</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table></div>
                <div class="cfs-note"><p><strong>When Windows' protection is not working.</strong> After some Windows updates, DPAPI refuses every request until the PC restarts. Studio then encrypts the token and OBS password with AES-256-GCM under the fallback key above, and moves them back to DPAPI by itself once it works again. Against other programs running as you, this is about as strong as DPAPI. Against someone who copies your whole disk, it is weaker, because the key sits next to the data instead of being tied to your Windows password. If even the key cannot be written, Studio keeps the secrets in memory only and asks for them again next time.</p></div>

                <h2 id="sent">Sent to CertiFried, and why</h2>
                <p>Everything goes over HTTPS or an encrypted WebSocket to the server address in Studio's Settings (certifriedmultitool.com unless you changed it).</p>
                <div class="cfs-table-wrap"><table class="cfs-table-stack">
                    <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>When</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>The pairing code or sign-in request, a device name (<code><%= _f.deviceName %></code> unless you type another), Studio's version and what it can do (OBS, sound, hotkeys)</td><td data-label="When">Once, when you pair</td><td data-label="Why">Add this PC to your Devices list. The PC name is only used as the default device name.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>The device token, and a small heartbeat</td><td data-label="When">On connect, then every 20 seconds or so</td><td data-label="Why">Prove the connection is yours and notice when it drops</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>OBS report: whether OBS is connected, its version, studio mode, the scene on air, the names of your scenes, of the sources in each, of your audio and other sources (with their type), of your filters, and the names of your sounds</td><td data-label="When">When it changes</td><td data-label="Why">Suggest these names in the automation editor and show the scene on air on Devices</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>VOD protection status: on or off and the reason (including that song requests are off, when Studio leaves OBS alone), which tracks are the live and VOD tracks, whether OBS is streaming or recording</td><td data-label="When">When it changes</td><td data-label="Why">Song requests play only while your VOD is protected</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Device report: the name, model and kind of each light Studio found, what it can do (colour, white, brightness) and its scene and effect names; Voicemeeter's type and version, its strip and bus labels, whether Macro Buttons is running and the names of the settings files in Documents\Voicemeeter; whether the Stream Deck connection is on and how many key pads are connected. Each light is named by an id made from its hardware id (its MAC address, serial number or the bridge's id), so automations keep working when its network address changes. Never a network address, a token or a file's contents</td><td data-label="When">When it changes, with the OBS report</td><td data-label="Why">Offer your lights, presets, strips and buses in the automation editor and on the Devices page</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>OBS events: scene changed (with the scene names), streaming, recording, replay buffer and virtual camera started or stopped, and the file name of a saved replay (not its folder)</td><td data-label="When">As they happen</td><td data-label="Why">They can start your automations</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>A voice phrase that matched and how close the match was (never the other words you said, unless you turn that on in Studio, Voice)</td><td data-label="When">When you say one of your phrases</td><td data-label="Why">Start the automation it belongs to</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>The words Whisper wrote down for a voice command you teach Studio, and words you mark as "not a command" (text only, saved on that automation; you can remove each one on the automation's page)</td><td data-label="When">When you use "Teach Studio how you say it", or "This was ..." or "Not a command" in the voice log (Studio 0.2.13 and later)</td><td data-label="Why">Recognise your commands the way you say them</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Heart rate: beats per minute, the time of the reading, whether the sensor is touching your skin, the time between beats when the device sends it, and the device's battery level</td><td data-label="When">At most once a second while the device you chose under Heart rate is connected and "Send it to CertiFried" is on (Studio 0.2.14 and later)</td><td data-label="Why">The Heart rate overlay, Heart rate automations and <code>{heart.bpm}</code>. The server keeps it in memory only (see below)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>The result of each action: done or failed, with a short reason and small details (the scene switched to, a screenshot's file name)</td><td data-label="When">After each action</td><td data-label="Why">Your Run history</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Requests for your automation list (which also says whether song requests are on), voice phrases, sound list, dock addresses, the music player address, your light presets and your channel counters; applying a light preset or changing a counter from a Stream Deck key; downloads of your dashboard sounds</td><td data-label="When">On connect and when they change</td><td data-label="Why">Hotkeys, voice commands, sounds, docks and song requests</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Update check: a request for <code>latest.yml</code>. No ID that tells your PC apart: from 0.2.5 the installed copy's update component (electron-updater) sends the same all-zero ID from every PC instead of a random one from <code>.updaterId</code> (CertiFried does not use staged roll-outs). Versions before 0.2.5 sent that random ID; CertiFried never read or stored it. The portable copy sends no ID.</td><td data-label="When">Shortly after start, then every <%= _f.updateHours %> hours, unless you turn it off</td><td data-label="Why">Offer new versions</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table></div>
                <p>Studio also opens your web browser on certifriedmultitool.com when you choose Sign in with CertiFried, Guide or Devices. It only opens addresses on certifriedmultitool.com.</p>

                <h2 id="never-leaves">Never leaves your PC</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>The pairing tokens of your Hue bridges and Nanoleaf panels, your lights' network addresses, and the Stream Deck token.</li>
                    <li>Your heart-rate device's name and Bluetooth address, and its readings while "Send it to CertiFried" is off. Studio reads the device directly; nothing goes through Pulsoid or your phone.</li>
                    <li>Your OBS WebSocket password. Use my OBS settings reads OBS's WebSocket settings file only when you click it, and stores the password like any other secret.</li>
                    <li>Microphone audio. Voice commands are recognised on your PC: a voice detector passes on only speech, and Whisper writes it down there (Windows speech recognition is used only when Whisper cannot run). Studio records nothing and saves no audio. The one exception is your choice: when you teach Studio a command, you can tick "Keep recordings on this PC", and then only those takes are saved, as files on your PC, never sent anywhere. The microphone is off until you turn it on.</li>
                    <li>Your log files, unless you send them to support yourself (Settings, Open log folder).</li>
                    <li>The contents of your sound files (only their names are reported), your screenshots, and your hotkey settings.</li>
                    <li>Your stream key, OBS's other settings, video, and anything else on your PC.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2 id="no-telemetry">No telemetry</h2>
                <p>Studio contains no analytics, usage tracking, crash reporting or advertising code. What we checked: its only dependencies are electron-updater (updates), obs-websocket-js (OBS), uiohook-napi (pressing and listening for keys), ws (the connection to CertiFried and to the Stream Deck plugin), multicast-dns (finding lights on your network), node-hid (Logitech Litra over USB), koffi (calling Voicemeeter's own Remote API on your PC) and onnxruntime-node (running the voice detector on your PC; Microsoft builds it with a usage-reporting option, which Studio switches off with ORT_DISABLE_TELEMETRY before it starts); Studio also ships two small Bluetooth helpers of its own (Govee lights, and heart rate from 0.2.14) that use Windows' built-in Bluetooth and have no network code; its window is not allowed to load anything from the internet; and the only addresses in its code are certifriedmultitool.com, OBS on your PC, the Stream Deck connection on 127.0.0.1, and, after you choose Find lights, the local network searches the light makers document (Govee's LAN discovery, mDNS for Hue, Nanoleaf and Elgato, LIFX's LAN broadcast) and the lights that answer. A sound action you write with an https address makes Studio download that address, which the site behind it can see. Errors are written to your local log and nowhere else. The source code is public, so you can check this yourself.</p>

                <h2 id="on-the-server">Kept on the server</h2>
                <div class="cfs-table-wrap"><table class="cfs-table-stack">
                    <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>How long</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>The device (name, app version, capabilities, last seen, a hash of the token)</td><td data-label="How long">Until you delete your data. Revoke stops it at once and it stays listed as revoked.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>The latest OBS report (each new one replaces it)</td><td data-label="How long">Until you delete your data</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>OBS events and voice phrases, in your event log</td><td data-label="How long">90 days</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Actions sent to Studio and their results</td><td data-label="How long">7 days</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Run history</td><td data-label="How long">30 days</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Pairing codes (as hashes; each works for 10 minutes)</td><td data-label="How long">1 day</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Sounds you upload to the dashboard</td><td data-label="How long">Until you delete them</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>The latest device report (lights, Voicemeeter, Stream Deck; replaced by each new one)</td><td data-label="How long">Until you delete your data</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Heart rate readings: in the server's memory only, for the current number, the graph (last 2 minutes) and this stream's low, average and high. Never written to the database or the event log</td><td data-label="How long">Until the next reading replaces it; the figures start again when you go live, 6 hours after the last reading, or when CertiFried restarts</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>When a Heart rate automation runs: the reading that started it, in that run's history (like any run)</td><td data-label="How long">30 days</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Your Pulsoid token, only if you add one on the Connections page (encrypted; never shown again or included in an export)</td><td data-label="How long">Until you remove it</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table></div>
                <p>The periods come from the retention table the server enforces every night. <a href="/legal/data">Data we collect</a> shows the live values for everything CertiFried keeps.</p>

                <h2 id="export-delete">Export and delete</h2>
                <p>Use the CertiFried bot's <code>/privacy</code> command in any server or in a direct message with the bot:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>/privacy export</code> sends you a file with your stream bot profile, devices (without token hashes), automations, the newest events and runs, the last OBS report and your sound names. Tokens and keys are never included.</li>
                    <li><code>/privacy delete</code> deletes your stream bot profile and everything under it, including your devices, events, runs and uploaded sound files. Studio on your PC then stops connecting; delete its folder too (see below).</li>
                </ul>
                <p>If you cannot use the command, email <a href="mailto:support@certifriedmultitool.com">support@certifriedmultitool.com</a>.</p>

                <h2 id="unpair">Unpair, revoke, uninstall</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Revoke</strong>: on the dashboard, Stream bot, <a href="/stream/bot/devices">Devices</a>, choose Revoke next to the PC. It is disconnected at once and its token stops working; the revoked entry is deleted 30 days later. <strong>Remove</strong> disconnects it and deletes it at once. Dock keys are revoked and removed the same way.</li>
                    <li><strong>Unpair</strong>: in Studio, Settings, Unpair this PC. The token is deleted from your PC. Revoke it on Devices too.</li>
                    <li><strong>Uninstall</strong>: Windows Settings, Apps. Uninstalling leaves your settings so a reinstall keeps them; to remove everything, also delete <code><%= _f.dataDir %></code> and <code><%= _f.screenshotsDir %></code>. Backups Studio made in OBS's folder end in <code>.certifried-backup-&lt;time&gt;</code> and can be deleted.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2 id="third-parties">Third parties</h2>
                <p>Studio itself talks to no third-party service, except a web address you put in a Play a sound action. Lights are controlled on your own network and USB, not through the makers' clouds; if you add a Govee or LIFX cloud key on the dashboard, the CertiFried server (not Studio) uses it to change those lights through Govee's or LIFX's service. Voice recognition runs on your PC. Heart rate is read straight from your device over Bluetooth. Only if you add a Pulsoid token on the Connections page does the CertiFried server (not Studio) connect to Pulsoid with it, to use Pulsoid's readings when your watch has sent nothing for 10 seconds. Our website is served through Cloudflare, so Studio's connections to certifriedmultitool.com pass through Cloudflare. What the CertiFried server shares with Twitch, Kick, tip services and music services is in the <a href="/privacy#information-sharing">Privacy Policy</a>.</p>

                <h2 id="changes">Changes to this page</h2>
                <p>When Studio starts sending something new, this page changes before the version that sends it is published.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>2026-11-19: First version, for Studio 0.2.4.</li>
                    <li>2026-11-19: Studio 0.2.6: lights, Voicemeeter and the Stream Deck plugin (what is kept, the device report, the new dependencies), and VOD protection only while song requests are on.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </main>
<%- include('partials/site-footer') %>
</body>
</html>
